@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-patch-values-must-be-json-values-checked-deeply-so-invalid-values-are-rejected-before-any-operation-runs
+type: decision-log
+title: "Patch values must be JSON values, checked deeply, so invalid values are rejected before any operation runs"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 defines a patch as a JSON document, so the `value` of an operation must be a JSON value. dl-003
+checks only that `value` is present, and the implementation accepts `undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity`,
+functions, `Date` objects and sparse arrays as values. Each of them is stored in the result, where it is
+not JSON and does not survive serialization. Users report that such patches are accepted, which the
+standard does not allow.
+
+This supersedes the sentence in dl-003 that says deep validation of `value` was not added.
+
+## Decision
+
+The `value` of `add`, `replace` and `test` is checked deeply during patch validation, before any
+operation runs. It must be a JSON value: `null`, a boolean, a finite number, a string, an array whose
+elements are all JSON values (no holes), or a plain object whose own members are all JSON values. Any
+other value throws, and the message names the operation index.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- An invalid value is rejected even when its operation would run last, as dl-003 already requires for
+  other invalid operations.
+- Validating a large value costs a walk over it, in addition to the copy made by dl-004.
+- Callers that build patches in code must pass JSON values; `undefined` no longer means "absent".
diff --git a/docs/memory/task/task-003-conform-applypatch-validation-and-errors-to-rfc-6902.md b/docs/memory/task/task-003-conform-applypatch-validation-and-errors-to-rfc-6902.md
